-- statement:
--   # Opp-bank BitNB variant-R stage-one word
--
--   This definition assembles a stage-one instruction word $W_{1F}$ for the densified opp-bank bit-network layout, with slot bound $NR=27794$, wire bound $NL=31359$, and flag-basis code bound $64251$. It is a new carrier, not a patch of the published word $\texttt{IntMul\_Kappa\_R8\_Data\_BitOppbankQHNetRec}$.
--
--   The word is the concatenation
--
--   $$
--   W_{1F}=\mathrm{preF}+\hspace{-0.25em}+\mathrm{copyoND}+\hspace{-0.25em}+\mathrm{segsR}+\hspace{-0.25em}+\mathrm{suffix}.
--   $$
--
--   The prefix $\mathrm{preF}$ is a low-code mix-forward, scatter, inject, and mix-inverse family, all at code $0$. The copy-out family $\mathrm{copyoND}$ writes the node code $c_{\mathrm{ND}}(t)=1773+t$ (not $c_{\mathrm{ND}}(t+1)$). The middle segment $\mathrm{segsR}$ is a mix-forward schedule over twenty-four blocks covering all $283$ chunks of the corrected GREC table, with sparse argument and geometry preloads spliced before selected mix instructions. Those preloads are stored as $242$ packed natural-number chunks ($\texttt{HITPACK}$); each hit is a $49$-bit triple $(\mathrm{index},\,\mathrm{code},\,\mathrm{wire})$. The suffix comprises a target-copy and scatter, an inject family at point codes, two high-code sweeps, a mix-inverse, and a copy-out at the high code $1$.
--
--   Return-node indices are read from the published $\texttt{RETN}$ table minus one, matching the densified identifier convention. Mix-forward blocks and preloads carry genuine node codes strictly below $64251$; the word is not a constant-low wipe.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The Lean namespace is `IntMul.Kappa.R8.Bit.Oppbank.QHNetRecR`. The public constant is $\texttt{W1F}$. The module imports $\texttt{WireLayout}$, $\texttt{LocSlots}$, and $\texttt{LocGrecR}$. It must not be imported in the same file as $\texttt{QHNet}$ or $\texttt{QHNetRec}$ (duplicate layout declarations). The constants $\texttt{segsR}$ and $\texttt{W1F}$ are marked irreducible so that later proofs do not unfold the full word.
